 "limitations": [
  "Today's weights applied to history: the record is what this book would have done, not what anyone held.",
  "GOOG (class C) stands in for GOOGL; the two trade within a fraction of a percent of each other.",
  "SPCX has traded since June 12, 2026. Before that its weight is spread over the other names pro rata, and its betas lean on TSLA's until it has two years of its own.",
  "The rates factor is a 20-year par bond rebuilt from the Treasury's daily par yields, a TLT proxy with duration about 12 against TLT's 16. Cash is proxied by the 2-year yield (older Treasury files here carry 2, 10 and 20 years only). That overstates what idle cash earned when the curve was steep (2021 and 2022) and understates it when the curve was inverted (2023 and 2024); a bill rate is the right series and is not in use yet.",
  "The drawdown cap is read from a constant-volatility simulation (geometric Brownian motion, 4,000 paths) at an assumed drift. It has no volatility clustering, no jumps and no change in correlation, so the 25% is a modelled 95th percentile under those assumptions and not a stop: a real drawdown at that size can be deeper. From v0.44 the page shows the size under fat tails, the book's own days in runs, volatility that moves, no equity premium and higher volatility (sensitivity.py), and whether the cap held in the walk-forward. The call still uses the constant-volatility number.",
  "The menu's rows are at or inside each limit exactly, in 5% steps of the short. The exact minimum short is reported beside the logged row; the logged row is the decision.",
  "Hedge legs are measured as futures: their returns are in excess of cash. No transaction costs, no margin, no borrow.",
  "Ten years of daily data hold one bear market (2022) and two fast crashes (2020, 2025); the tail numbers are only as wide as that record, which is why the most cautious of three methods governs."
 ],
 "meta": {
  "source": "mirror download"
 },
